  "title": "Extinction Learning: Rewiring Fear",
  "emoji": "❤️",
  "color": "#9333EA",
  "description": "A quick, practical guide to Extinction Learning: Rewiring Fear.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A soldier returns home safe but flinches at every car backfire. The war is over, but the amygdala has not received the memo.",
      "visual": "🎆"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Phobias form when the amygdala pairs a neutral stimulus with danger through classical conditioning, sometimes in a single trial.",
      "visual": "⚡"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Exposure therapy works by creating a new memory in the prefrontal cortex competes with and suppresses the old fear association.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The original fear memory is never deleted. It is overridden, which is why phobias can return under high stress.",
      "visual": "📂"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Researchers found that brief exposure during memory reconsolidation windows can weaken the original fear trace permanently.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You cannot unlearn fear.",
      "visual": "🏆"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "How does exposure therapy reduce phobias?",
    "options": [
      "It creates a new competing memory that suppresses the fear association",
      "It permanently deletes the original fear memory",
      "It numbs the amygdala so it cannot respond"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
